The Security Council,
Guided by the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations, and reaffirming in particular the inadmissibility of the acquisition of territory by force,
Reaffirming its relevant resolutions, including resolutions 242 (1967), 338 (1973), 1397 (2002), 1515 (2003), 1850 (2008) and 2334 (2016),
Reaffirming the obligation of all parties to respect international humanitarian law, including the Fourth Geneva Convention relative to the Protection of Civilian Persons in Time of War, of 12 August 1949,
Recalling the advisory opinion rendered on 9 July 2004 by the International Court of Justice,
Expressing grave concern at the recent escalation of tensions and violence in the Middle East, including in Jerusalem, the West Bank and Gaza, and at the loss of civilian lives,
Condemning all acts of violence against civilians, including acts of terror, as well as all acts of provocation, incitement and destruction,
Reaffirming its vision of a region where two democratic States, Israel and Palestine, live side by side in peace within secure and recognized borders,
Reaffirming that Jerusalem is a final status issue to be resolved through negotiations between the parties consistent with relevant United Nations resolutions and prior agreements,
Stressing the need to respect the historic status quo at the Holy Sites in Jerusalem and the importance of preserving the multi-religious character of the city,
Emphasizing the importance of all parties refraining from actions and rhetoric that undermine trust and the prospects for peace,
Welcoming the continued efforts of the Secretary-General and regional and international partners to de-escalate tensions and revive credible negotiations,
Stressing that the status quo is not sustainable and that significant steps are urgently needed to stabilize the situation and create conditions for successful final status negotiations,

1. Calls for immediate de-escalation, calm and restraint, and for all parties to cease acts of violence and incitement;
2. Condemns all acts of violence against civilians, including acts of terror, and calls for accountability for those responsible;
3. Reaffirms its commitment to the two-State solution based on the 1967 lines and to the vision of two States, Israel and Palestine, living side by side in peace within secure and recognized borders;
4. Reaffirms that Jerusalem is a final status issue to be resolved through negotiations between the parties, and calls upon all parties to refrain from actions that could prejudge the outcome of final status negotiations;
5. Stresses the need to respect the historic status quo at the Holy Sites in Jerusalem and calls for the preservation of the multi-religious character of the city;
6. Calls upon all parties to respect international humanitarian law and to refrain from provocative actions, incitement and inflammatory rhetoric;
7. Urges the parties, with the support of the international community, to resume credible negotiations on all final status issues without delay;
8. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council within 60 days on the implementation of this resolution and on efforts to de-escalate tensions and revive the peace process;
9. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

The Security Council,
Guided by the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations, and reaffirming in particular the inadmissibility of the acquisition of territory by force,
Reaffirming its relevant resolutions, including resolutions 242 (1967), 338 (1973), 476 (1980), 478 (1980), 1397 (2002), 1515 (2003), 1850 (2008) and 2334 (2016),
Reaffirming the obligation of Israel, the occupying Power, to abide scrupulously by the Fourth Geneva Convention relative to the Protection of Civilian Persons in Time of War, of 12 August 1949, and the obligation of all parties to respect international humanitarian law,
Recalling the advisory opinion rendered on 9 July 2004 by the International Court of Justice,
Reaffirming that the establishment by Israel of settlements in the Palestinian territory occupied since 1967, including East Jerusalem, has no legal validity and constitutes a flagrant violation under international law, and reiterating its demand that Israel immediately and completely cease all settlement activities and fully respect its legal obligations in this regard, consistent with resolution 2334 (2016),
Expressing grave concern at the recent escalation of tensions and violence in the Middle East, including in Jerusalem, the West Bank and Gaza, and at the loss of civilian lives,
Condemning all acts of violence against civilians, including acts of terror, as well as all acts of provocation, incitement and destruction,
Reaffirming its vision of a region where two democratic States, Israel and Palestine, live side by side in peace within secure and recognized borders,
Expressing deep regret at recent decisions concerning the status of Jerusalem, which risk prejudging the outcome of final status negotiations and undermine the prospects for a two-State solution,
Reaffirming that Jerusalem is a final status issue to be resolved through negotiations between the parties consistent with relevant United Nations resolutions and prior agreements,
Stressing the need to respect the historic status quo at the Holy Sites in Jerusalem and the importance of preserving the multi-religious character of the city,
Emphasizing the importance of all parties refraining from actions and rhetoric that undermine trust and the prospects for peace,
Welcoming the continued efforts of the Secretary-General and regional and international partners, including the Quartet, Egypt and the Russian Federation, to de-escalate tensions and revive credible negotiations,
Stressing that the status quo is not sustainable and that significant steps are urgently needed to stabilize the situation and create conditions for successful final status negotiations,

1. Calls for immediate de-escalation, calm and restraint, and for all parties to cease acts of violence and incitement;
2. Condemns all acts of violence against civilians, including acts of terror, and calls for accountability for those responsible;
3. Reaffirms its commitment to the two-State solution based on the 4 June 1967 lines, including with regard to Jerusalem, and to the vision of two States, Israel and Palestine, living side by side in peace within secure and recognized borders, and underlines that it will not recognize any changes to those lines other than those agreed by the parties through negotiations;
4. Demands that Israel immediately and completely cease all settlement activities in the occupied Palestinian territory, including East Jerusalem, and fully respect its legal obligations in this regard, consistent with resolution 2334 (2016);
5. Reaffirms that any decisions and actions which purport to have altered the character, status or demographic composition of the Holy City of Jerusalem have no legal effect, are null and void and must be rescinded in compliance with relevant resolutions of the Security Council; reaffirms that Jerusalem is a final status issue to be resolved through negotiations between the parties; and calls upon all parties to refrain from actions that could prejudge the outcome of final status negotiations;
6. Calls upon all States to refrain from the establishment of diplomatic missions in the Holy City of Jerusalem, pursuant to resolution 478 (1980), to comply with Security Council resolutions regarding Jerusalem, and not to recognize any actions or measures contrary to those resolutions;
7. Stresses the need to respect the historic status quo at the Holy Sites in Jerusalem and calls for the preservation of the multi-religious character of the city;
8. Calls upon all parties to respect international humanitarian law and to refrain from provocative actions, incitement and inflammatory rhetoric;
9. Urges the parties, with the support of the international community, to resume credible direct negotiations on all final status issues without delay, without preconditions, on the basis of the Madrid principles, the Arab Peace Initiative and the Quartet Roadmap, with the active support of the Quartet and regional partners, and taking into account Israel's legitimate security concerns and the Palestinian right to statehood;
10. Welcomes and encourages efforts by the Quartet and regional partners, including Egypt and the Russian Federation, to facilitate the resumption of direct Palestinian-Israeli negotiations;
11. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council within 90 days on the implementation of this resolution and on efforts to de-escalate tensions and revive the peace process;
12. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

